I've managed to run a couple of klipper commands to get a list of human readable microcontroller commands, created with Klipper's batch mode and a basic AVR based Klipper config file and klipper.dict.
See examples/klipper/slideprinter/draw_squares_microcontroller_commands_commands.txt for the result.

So these Klipper related commands work on my desktop machine now:

```bash
# For batch mode we need a dict. Make an AVR one because we want to simulate extruder which the "Linux process" dict won't allow
# `make menuconfig`
# Choose AVR
# Save and exit
# `make`

~/klippy-env/bin/python ./klippy/klippy.py ~/repos/hp-sim5/examples/klipper/slideprinter/printer-slideprinter-avr.cfg  -i /home/torbjorn/repos/hp-sim5/public/examples/gcode/draw_squares.gcode  -o test.serial -v -d out/klipper.dict
~/klippy-env/bin/python ./klippy/parsedump.py /home/torbjorn/repos/klipper/out/klipper.dict test.serial > test.txt
```

Now klippy is only a part of the data pipeline I'm trying to create. Here's an overview of that pipeline:
gcode -> web browser -> Octoprint -> /tmp/printer (virtual serial port created by Klipper) -> klippy (sudo service klipper start) and with our config file printer-slideprinter-avr.cfg -> serial (eg /dev/serial/by-id/usb-1a86_USB2.0-Serial-if00-port0, baud 250000) -> microcontroller that we need to emulate

The current Slideprinter example gets its command via websocket or webworker.
See examples/js/slideprinter/moveCommander.js for details on how that works in js, or see python/slideprinter/move_commander.py for a Python example that only uses websockets.

We basically want to replace the MoveCommander in those two examples with real Klipper instead.
In particular, we want the output of Klipper to be captured by js running in examples/js/slideprinter/index.html
and passed on to the remote spool system similar to how the websocket and webworker routes currently work, a new
if-statement or other event structure ending with something like
`remoteSpoolSystem.addCommand(e.data.command);`.

Inside the MoveCommander this was quite easy since we could just invent a protocol and send the commands in clear text with
`await this.sendCommand(interpolated_cmd);`


Klipper however will be using its internal protocol. We must create a module KlipperHandler that's able to capture the Klipper output, maybe translate it to the current working json driven protocol ({"action": "gcode", "command": [type: "Move"...etc]}), and feed the currently working pipeline from there.

First, let's establish the KlipperHandler module without translating anything, just print the received bytes to the console so we know we're getting something.

Also modify the klipper command to read the gcode and stream commands to our stubbed serial port that we need KlipperHandler to create (I don't know if klippy.py can do this or if we need to launch the klipper service, or get our hands on a klipper executable or something). Look into ai_docs/Klipper_Debugging_tools.md and maybe ai_docs/Klipper_on_rpi and ai_docs/Klipper_installation for details on how we achieve this on our local desktop machine with Klipper.


